@page "/mechanism/4-dotnet-object-reference"

<PageTitle>4. DotNetObjectReference — JS interop demos</PageTitle>

<div class="mechanism-page">
    <MechanismNav />

    <header class="mech-head">
        <p class="mech-kicker">
            Mechanism 4 of @Mechanisms.All.Count · you are reading this in
            <span class="host-chip">@RuntimeMode.Name</span> — the same page in
            <CounterpartLink />
        </p>
        <h1>DotNetObjectReference: JavaScript calling back into C#</h1>
        <p class="mech-lede">
            The only supported way for a JavaScript event to run a C# method. You create a handle to a
            C# object, pass it into a JavaScript call, and JavaScript invokes
            <code>[JSInvokable]</code> methods on it. The handle pins the C# object in memory, so
            whoever created it has to release it.
        </p>
        <p class="demo-support">
            Available in: <strong>@Mechanisms.Availability(4)</strong>
        </p>
    </header>

    <section class="mech-block">
        <h2>The identifier is the contract, not the method name</h2>
        <p>
            <code>[JSInvokable]</code> accepts an optional identifier. When you give it one, that
            identifier <strong>replaces</strong> the C# method name — it does not add an alias
            alongside it. This app registers one method as
            <code>[JSInvokable("Demo.Shared")]</code>, and calling it by its C# name from JavaScript
            fails, which is the second ✗ panel below.
        </p>
        <p>
            The practical consequence: renaming the C# method is a silent breaking change to
            JavaScript you cannot compile-check. Search for the identifier, not the method name.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mech-block">
        <h2>What the panels print in @RuntimeMode.Name</h2>
        <p class="mech-out-label">✓ Call the method by its registered name</p>
        <pre class="mech-out">the callback ran; C# saw it. HitCount in C# is now 1.</pre>

        <p class="mech-out-label">✗ Give the method a custom identifier, then call its C# name</p>
        <pre class="mech-out mech-out-fail">JSException: System.ArgumentException: The type 'AnimationCallback' does not
contain a public invokable method with
[JSInvokableAttribute("OnAnimationEndWithCustomIdentifier")].</pre>

        <p class="mech-out-label">✗ Dispose the handle in JavaScript, then call it again</p>
        @if (RuntimeMode.IsWebAssembly)
        {
            <pre class="mech-out mech-out-fail">JSException: System.ArgumentException: There is no tracked object with id '5'.
Perhaps the DotNetObjectReference instance was already disposed.
Arg_ParamName_Name, dotNetObjectId</pre>
        }
        else
        {
            <pre class="mech-out mech-out-fail">JSException: System.ArgumentException: There is no tracked object with id '10'.
Perhaps the DotNetObjectReference instance was already disposed.
(Parameter 'dotNetObjectId')</pre>
        }

        <p class="mech-note">
            Two things to read in that last one. It is an <code>ArgumentException</code>, not an
            <code>ObjectDisposedException</code>, and it does not name your type — the runtime only
            knows the id is gone. And the tail of the message is host-specific: WebAssembly prints
            <code>Arg_ParamName_Name, dotNetObjectId</code>, the resource key, while Blazor Server
            prints <code>(Parameter 'dotNetObjectId')</code>, the formatted text. Same .NET exception,
            two renderings, so quote one of them as host-specific.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mech-block">
        <h2>Run these three panels one at a time</h2>
        <p>
            The first two panels share one handle, created fresh for each run and disposed when the
            component goes away. Press two <em>Run it</em> buttons back to back on Blazor Server and
            the ✓ panel starts failing with the "no tracked object" message above — not because
            disposal is broken, but because the second click released the handle while the first
            call's round trip was still in flight. That accident is the mechanism's real hazard,
            demonstrated without the demo trying to.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mech-split">
        <div class="mech-use">
            <h2>Use it when</h2>
            <ul>
                <li>JavaScript must notify C# — animation end, timer, DOM event — and there is no
                    other way to get the signal across the boundary.</li>
                <li>The callback is one-shot and you dispose it the moment it fires, with a
                    <code>Dispose</code> on the C# side as the safety net.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="mech-never">
            <h2>Never when</h2>
            <ul>
                <li>You create one per frame or per repeated event.</li>
                <li>C# can poll the state through a module function instead. A callback is the one
                    interop object whose lifetime is controlled from the side that has no garbage
                    collector.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>
    </section>

    <h2 class="mech-live-head">The live panels</h2>
    <CascadingValue Name="MechanismPageOwnsHeading" Value="true">
        <Demo04Callbacks />
    </CascadingValue>

    <MechanismPager Number="4" />
</div>
